<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Louis Randon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marshal Randon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Louis Randon (1795–1871) was a French general and marshal who served as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> from 1851 to 1858. His campaign of 1857 subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-kabylia">Great Kabylia</a>, completing the conquest of the mountains of northern Algeria, and he founded Fort-Napoléon there. He was later minister of war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-louis-randon.jpg" alt="Jacques Louis Randon" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Léon Crémière · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RANDON2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacques Louis César Alexandre Randon was born on 25 March 1795 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a> in Dauphiné into a Protestant family; later in life, under the influence of his second wife, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. He was a nephew of General Jean-Gabriel, Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marchand-dessalines">Marchand</a>, and the revolutionary Antoine-Joseph Barnave was a first cousin of his mother.</p><p>He enlisted in the French Army at sixteen, joined his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marchand-dessalines">Marchand</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and was promoted to sergeant on 11 April 1812. He took part in the Russian campaign, the taking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the retreat, and then fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the battles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> among others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Randon was Minister of War from January to October 1851 and was then appointed Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, holding the post from 11 December 1851 to 31 August 1858. At first he did not favour the coup of December 1851, but he came to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a>. In 1852 he was made a count, by reversion of the title of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marchand-dessalines">Marchand</a>, and in 1856 he became a Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the same time as Canrobert and Bosquet. He served again as Minister of War from 1859 to 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Because of his age he received no command in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870. He was nevertheless held partly responsible for the French defeat, accused of neglecting preparations during his second ministry and of dissuading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> from acting on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s side at the time of the Battle of Sadova in 1866. Modern research has shown the second charge to be unfounded: Randon had in fact urged immediate action against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><p>Randon died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 16 January 1871, leaving one surviving daughter. His first wife, Clotilde Périer, was a sister of Casimir Périer, President of the Council under the July Monarchy and grandfather of Jean Casimir-Périer, President of the French Republic; he later married Zénaïde Suin. In 1859 the botanist Ernest Saint-Charles Cosson named Randonia, a genus of flowering plants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the family Resedaceae, in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Louis Randon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.19,5.73&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Grenoble on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4686 569 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques Louis Randon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5136 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5136 839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.19,5.73&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
